"""ashenvale story pass (22 Nov 2026), to docs/quest_writing_standard.md. Where the giver's speech sat in the accept
line and the journal showed a summary or narration, the speech is now the description and the accept line a new short
beat; the rest were written fresh. Turn-ins untouched."""

STORY = {
    'ashenvale_grizzly_problem': (
        "Bears keep to the trees. A bear wants cover and distance and no part of whatever we're doing. That's what a bear is.\n\nThese ones come down to the lake and stand in the open and watch this post. They aren't pacing and they aren't feeding. They stand facing us for as long as it suits them, and then they go back up.\n\nI've held Maestra's Post for eleven years, and I've never once been watched by something that couldn't tell me why.\n\nPut the boldest of them down before one of them walks into Astranaar's market and we find out what the next stage of this is.",
        'The boldest ones first, the ones that stand and watch you.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_timberling_bark': (
        "Look at the posts. Go on, the carved ones at the corners. Do you see the splits running with the grain?\n\nThose are the wards, and they're the only reason this post sleeps at night. Heartwood holds a ward the way nothing I can requisition does. I've tried iron, I've tried stone, and I've tried asking Astranaar nicely, which was the least effective of the three.\n\nThe timberlings in Silverwind Vale carry it in their chests.\n\nAnd before you ask me to be gentle about it, they came through my fence line last spring and they weren't gentle with it, and I've a very long memory for fences.",
        "The heartwood's in their chests. You'll have to go in for it.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_ursal_the_mauler': (
        "Shalasyr sends word the totems are done, which is what I needed. Then whatever was feeding the Highland bears has stopped, and Ursal has nothing left driving him but his own temper, which, to be fair to him, has always been considerable.\n\nHe's the biggest of them, and he's the one herding the others down toward the lake. That's been the shape of it all along. He moves, and they move.\n\nGo and finish it. And do something for me. Before you do it, get a proper look at his eyes, and then come back and tell me honestly whether they were a bear's. I've asked three people that question, and every one of them changed the subject.\n\nHe dens in the Ragged Highlands, east of this post.",
        'Look at his eyes before you finish it, and tell me honestly.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_sentinel_scouts_report': (
        "Two of mine went up the ridge road toward the Ragged Highlands nine days ago.\n\nThey're good, and I want that understood before anything else, because the first thing anyone says is that scouts go missing, and I won't have theirs be the generation that gets shrugged at. They're careful, they're experienced, and I'd have heard from them by now if there were anything at all to hear.\n\nRetrace their route, all of it, as far as the Highlands themselves.\n\nI don't need heroics, and I don't want a rescue attempt. I need to know the point on that road where it stops being safe, because at the moment I'm sending people past it without knowing where it is.",
        'Walk it as far as the Highlands, and mark where it stops being safe.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_furbolg_ravagers': (
        "I want to tell you something about the furbolg before you go up that ridge, because nobody your age was taught it.\n\nWe've shared this forest with them for a very long time. We were never that formal about it. We both knew where the other's ground was, and in all that time there wasn't a drop of blood between us.\n\nNow the ravagers hold the ridge, and they attack anything that crosses it. They've stopped distinguishing.\n\nSomething has changed them, and I've got to get scouts in there to find out what. Thin the ravagers so that becomes possible, and while you're among them, look at them properly. You'll see what I mean.\n\nThe ravagers hold the Ragged Highlands, north of Astranaar, past the lake and the Moonwell, in the north-east corner of the forest.",
        "Look at them properly while you're among them.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_furbolg_shaman_totems': (
        "The ravagers aren't the cause. I've been certain of that since the second report, and now I can prove it.\n\nThey're being driven from behind. The shamans plant totems in the dens, and the madness is always worst where the totems are thickest. It isn't worst where the fighting is, or at the ridge. It's worst in the dens, at the carvings.\n\nBring me some of those totems out. And carry them in cloth. I mean that as an order rather than advice. I won't have you holding one of those bare-handed across half a forest to spare yourself the trouble of a wrapping.\n\nThe shamans keep to the Ragged Highlands with the rest, north of Astranaar in the north-east corner of the forest.",
        "Wrap the totems before you carry them. That's an order.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_keeper_rustveil': (
        "Rustveil kept the Moonwhisper Grove before Astranaar had walls. He kept it longer than that, from before there was anyone here to need walls.\n\nHe taught me my first song. I was small enough that he had to bend down to it, and he was patient in a way that nothing that old has any reason to be.\n\nSomething has turned him against the forest he watched. I've been to see for myself, and I won't describe it to you, partly to spare you and mostly to spare me.\n\nI'm asking you to put him down. I'm asking it knowing exactly what I'm asking, and I won't dress it up as mercy or duty.\n\nDo it quickly. If anything in him can still know what's happening, I don't want him knowing it for long.",
        "Do it quickly. If he can still know, I don't want him knowing for long.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_wisp_lights': (
        "The wisps are the Grove's lanterns. That isn't poetry. It's their function, and it's why the Grove is lit at all.\n\nThey're old, older than the trees in some cases, and there's an argument, which I happen to believe, that they remember who planted this place.\n\nThey're flickering, not dying out, the way a lamp does when something is wrong with the fuel rather than the wick, and I want the healers to have something to work from before it becomes the other thing.\n\nBring me what falls from them as they fade. And listen to me carefully: don't take a mote that hasn't fallen, not one. I'll know, and so will the Grove, and I'm genuinely unsure which of us would be worse about it.",
        "Only the motes that fall on their own, and I'll know if you don't.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_refugees_belongings': (
        "I know how this sounds. I came off the east coast with a wagon and I lost the wagon, and what I'm asking a stranger to go and find is a piece of jewellery.\n\nIt's only silver. It's a painted picture, badly painted, by somebody in a market who charged too much.\n\nIt's the only picture of my sister that exists.\n\nThe wagon went over on the Night Run, and a cat had it out of the box before I was back on my feet. I'm not asking for the rest. The rest was pots and clothes, and I've made my peace with pots.\n\nPlease, just the locket.",
        'Just the locket, please.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_nightsaber_thinning': (
        "I keep seeing their eyes when I close mine, the cats, in the dark, around the wagon while we were still getting out from under it.\n\nI'm not going to pretend this is about my locket. You've already brought that back, and I'm not owed anything else.\n\nIt's that there'll be another family on that road next week. The Night Run is the only way through for anyone coming off the coast, and they'll be doing exactly what we did, at night, because that's when you're told it's safest.\n\nThin the stalkers before the next wagon tries it.\n\nI'll sleep better. I think rather a lot of people will, and most of them will never know there was anything to sleep badly about, which seems to me the best possible outcome.",
        'Before the next wagon tries the Night Run, please.',
    ),
    'splintertree_burning_blade_scouts': (
        "Cultists poke about the Wastes like rats in a cellar, and like rats they're only interesting for what they lead you to.\n\nThe Burning Blade are putting up ritual circles out there. I can't find them. The Wastes are big and a circle is small, and these people aren't stupid about where they put them.\n\nBut I don't need to find the circles. I need to find the guards, and guards stand near the thing they're guarding, which is the single most reliable fact in the practice of war.\n\nKill their scouts along the Wastes' edge. Wherever you have to kill a lot of them in one place, that's a circle, and then I can finish my map.",
        "Wherever you have to kill a lot of them at once, that's a circle.",
    ),
    'splintertree_charred_wolves': (
        "Used to be, a wolf ran from a fire. You could set a line and the whole forest would go the other way and that was something you could rely on.\n\nThese come out of the burn like they're from it. Straight through the black stretch at a dead run, into the open, at a line of armed orcs.\n\nThey've had two of my patrols bleeding and they've had the outpost's livestock, and I'll be honest with you that I mind the patrols more but the quartermaster minds the livestock.\n\nThin the pack. And while you're in there, find me whatever's making them burn-mad, because a wolf doesn't decide to do this on its own any more than it decides to walk into a fire.",
        "Find what's making them burn-mad while you're in there.",
    ),
    'splintertree_fel_imp_cache': (
        "They take hinges, buckles, a strip of my own mess-tent canvas, cut square.\n\nThe imps carry off the dumbest things out of this outpost and stack them in the dirt out in the Wastes, and I've been going out there to look at the stacks, which I'd rather you didn't mention to the War Mage.\n\nIt isn't a nest. I've seen nests and I've seen hoards, and this is neither. It's arranged, the same way every time, and a thief doesn't arrange.\n\nI think it's a message, and I think nobody told us how to read it. Bring me scraps off them, enough that I can lay it out the way they do and look at the whole of it at once.\n\nThe imps nest in the Demon Wastes, east of here. Whatever they're carrying, I want it back.",
        'Bring enough scraps that I can lay the whole thing out.',
    ),
    'splintertree_satyr_ritual': (
        "The satyrs at Satyr's Throne have been singing for three nights.\n\nI've fought a great many things in this forest, and I've learned their songs the way you learn any language, by listening to it being used for ordinary purposes until the unusual ones stand out.\n\nThat song isn't a welcome. It isn't mourning either, which was my second guess and which I'd have preferred.\n\nIt's a song of preparation. Something is being made ready at the Throne, and the tricksters are leading the chant, which means the tricksters are the part that has to be carried.\n\nRemove them, and the song has nothing to carry it, and whatever is being prepared stays unprepared a while longer.",
        'The tricksters carry the chant. Take them, and the song falls apart.',
    ),
    'splintertree_lord_malgan': (
        "A tyrant who loses a ritual has two choices open to him. He can try again, or die trying.\n\nI've buried enough grunts to know which one Malgan will pick, and I've no intention of letting him get far enough into it to find out whether I'm right.\n\nHe holds the Throne by fear now rather than by strength. Those aren't the same thing, and the satyrs under him know it even if he doesn't. You can hear it in how they move when he's in the open.\n\nSo I'll make the choice for him. He's at the Throne. Go and take it away from him.\n\nSatyr's Throne is north of Splintertree. He hasn't left it, and he won't.",
        "He won't leave the Throne. Go and take it away from him.",
    ),
    'splintertree_twilight_acolytes': (
        "Something under the Barrow is listening, and the acolytes are the ones doing the talking.\n\nThey go out at night and whisper to the buried dead. They don't chant. They whisper, the way you'd talk to somebody through a door, and you can stand on the mound and hear the shape of it without catching a word.\n\nI don't know what's down there. I want to be extremely clear that I don't intend to find out by letting the conversation continue until it answers.\n\nSilence the acolytes. Whatever is under that mound can go back to being a thing nobody has spoken to in a thousand years.\n\nThe Twilight Barrow is north-east of Splintertree, at the eastern edge of the forest.",
        "Silence them before whatever's under the mound answers.",
    ),
    'splintertree_twilight_overseer': (
        "Every cult has somebody in it who isn't a believer, only a boss.\n\nYou can pick him out at a distance if you know what to look for. He's the one who doesn't look up during the important part. The acolytes were whispering for somebody, and it was him.\n\nKill him and the rest scatter, or they come for you, which is a better fight than the one we'll be having if whatever is under that Barrow learns to speak for itself.\n\nI'd rather fight a hundred frightened cultists than one patient thing underground with a voice.\n\nYou'll find him where you found the acolytes, at the Twilight Barrow, north-east of Splintertree.",
        "He's patient, so don't give him time to be.",
    ),
    'splintertree_pit_lord_emissary': (
        "It arrived with terms.\n\nIt's been standing in the Wastes for two days waiting for somebody of appropriate rank to come out and receive them, and in that time three of my scouts have gone close enough to hear the opening and come back changed in ways I'm still assessing.\n\nI don't read the terms of things that stand in fire. There's no version of this where we hear it out and come away with more than we started with. An emissary's entire function is to make the next thing sound reasonable.\n\nCut it off before it finishes a sentence, preferably the first one.\n\nIt's waiting in the Demon Wastes, east of the post, for our answer. Take it one.",
        'Cut it off before it finishes its first sentence.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_night_run_watch': (
        "Eltharaen keeps asking me what's on the Night Run. She asks it in that patient way she has, as though I've simply not got round to finding out.\n\nAnd I keep telling her I don't know, and every time I say it I like myself a little less.\n\nThe wagons die on that road. That's the extent of our intelligence. That's what I've got to send up to Astranaar, that wagons die there, which anyone could have told me from the wreckage.\n\nWalk it for me, end to end, all of it. And tell me what that road looks like to someone who isn't afraid of it, because I'm no longer certain I can see it clearly.\n\nThe Night Run is the old road in the south-east corner of these woods, a long way from this post, down past the Moonwell and the lake, then east.",
        "Walk it as if you weren't afraid of it, and tell me what you see.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_wraith_toll': (
        "Now I know what's on it, I can tell you what's wrong with it.\n\nThe dead of the Night Run don't want revenge. People assume they do, because people assume a hostile dead thing must have a grievance, and it makes the road easier to think about.\n\nThey want company, and nothing more. Every wagon that goes down that road is a crowd of warm people passing through a place where something is extremely alone, and they take a toll because taking is the only way they know to ask.\n\nUnderstanding it doesn't help the carters. End the ones on the road, and the rest might remember they were people and settle.",
        'End the ones on the road. The rest may remember what they were.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_commander_spiders': (
        "I don't enjoy asking this of you, and I'd rather say that at the front than have it sit between us for the whole conversation.\n\nMy people are stretched from the coast to the Highlands, and you're here. And the forest spiders on the trade road don't care in the slightest whose wagons they take. They've had Sentinel supply and they've had Horde supply, and I understand they took a goblin caravan last month, which is the only part of this I've enjoyed.\n\nSo clear the road.\n\nAnd I'll remember that it was asked and that it was answered. In this forest, that has lately been most of what we have.",
        "Clear the road, and I'll remember it was done.",
    ),
    'ashenvale_dryads_gone_quiet': (
        "The dryads answer when I sing. It isn't a courtesy or training. It's simply how it's always worked here, for as long as there's been a Grove to sing in.\n\nThey haven't answered for a week.\n\nI've been down there every evening. I've sung every call they answer, and last night I sang one I'm not supposed to use outside of need, and nothing came.\n\nThe corruption is in them now. It won't leave on its own, and it won't leave because I ask it to. I've established both of those at some cost to my dignity.\n\nI'm sorry to ask you to release them. I'm sorrier that I can't do it myself without my voice being the last thing they hear.\n\nMoonwhisper Grove lies east of Astranaar, near the border of the eastern woods. Go and see what has stopped them answering.",
        'Be gentle with them, if you can.',
    ),
    'ashenvale_lake_survey': (
        "I keep a ledger of this lake's moods. It isn't a poetic exercise. The Moon Guard have kept one here for generations, and the entries are mostly about weather.\n\nI've counted the ripples from the tower for eleven nights running.\n\nThey don't follow the wind, not once in eleven nights, and on two of those nights there was no wind at all and there were still ripples, and they were moving toward the shore rather than along it.\n\nI'd like a second pair of eyes on that shoreline, preferably eyes that aren't mine, because I've been looking at this for eleven nights and I've started to doubt them, and a Moon Guard who doubts his own observations should go and get somebody else's.",
        "Look at the shoreline carefully, with eyes that aren't tired.",
    ),
    'zoram_murloc_nets': (
        "I've fished this strand for forty years.\n\nIn all that time the murlocs never touched a line of mine. We had an arrangement, near enough. They worked the shallows, I worked the deep, and if one of them got into the net he came out again and we both pretended it hadn't happened.\n\nNow they're cutting my nets, with bone knives, deliberately, from underneath, in the dark, and leaving the fish.\n\nThey aren't hungry. That's the part that's had me awake. A hungry murloc takes the fish.\n\nDeal with the spearfishers for me. And when you're back I'll tell you the story of the big silverfin, which is an extremely good story, and which nobody on this coast will sit still for any more.",
        "Come back afterwards for the silverfin story. It's a good one.",
    ),
    'zoram_crab_traps': (
        "The crabs got bold when the naga started wandering the shallows.\n\nThat's the order it happened in, and I'll stand by it. The naga came, everything else in the water had a worse thing to worry about than me, and now the big shore crabs take my traps and crush them against the rocks for no reason I can determine beyond that they can.\n\nI've been rebuilding traps for a month. I'm an old man who spends his days making boxes for crabs to destroy, which wasn't the retirement I'd sketched out.\n\nThin out the big ones. Then I'll be able to eat dinner like a free man instead of like a crab's tenant.",
        'The big ones, the ones that crack the traps.',
    ),
    'zoram_naga_coastguard': (
        "I build a boat in a week, and they sink it in a night.\n\nThat's three this month. I'm most of the way through a fourth, and I've reached the point where I'm building it slowly on purpose, because finishing it means finding out whether it lasts till morning.\n\nThe coastguards come in off the reef after dark. They don't take anything and they don't come ashore. They hole the hulls and go back out.\n\nI'm not asking you to be brave about it. Bravery's no use to me. I had a nephew who was brave about it, and I'd rather have had the boat and the nephew.\n\nI'm asking you to be efficient. Drive them off the reef.",
        'Be efficient, not brave. Drive them off the reef.',
    ),
    'zoram_tidecaller_pearls': (
        "Here's the thing nobody at Zoram'gar will sit still long enough to hear.\n\nThe tidecallers don't carry weapons, not one. I've been close enough to be sure of it, closer than I'd choose to be again.\n\nThey carry pearls, and the water answers them.\n\nI build boats. That means I spend my life working out what water will do and planning around it, and I'm telling you water isn't supposed to do what it does when one of those things holds a pearl up.\n\nBring some back. I want to know what a pearl is made of that it can do that, and whether it keeps doing it once it's out of a naga's hand. If it does, I can stop building boats that sink and start building ones that argue back.",
        'Bring the pearls back whole. I want to see if they keep doing it.',
    ),
    'zoram_lookout_bluffs': (
        "I watch the water so the rest of them can sleep. That's the posting, and I've never resented it.\n\nI watch the water, and lately the water watches back. I've had several days to find a better way of saying it, and that's still the most accurate one available.\n\nHere's my actual problem, stripped of all that. I can't see the Coast Bluffs from this post. There's a shoulder of rock in the way and there always has been, and for years that didn't matter.\n\nI think that's where it's coming from. Go up there and walk them.\n\nAnd if it's nothing, if it's birds and surf and a tired Sentinel, then come back and tell me that, and I'll go to sleep for about a day and a half.",
        "If it's only birds and surf, tell me that too.",
    ),
    'splintertree_timber_quota': (
        "Orgrimmar wants timber, and I want my loggers alive. For most of my career those have been the same objective, and lately they haven't been.\n\nThe grizzlies have decided the stand north-west of here, up at the Warsong Lumber Camp, is theirs. They come in at the crews, in daylight, which bears don't do unless something is very wrong with the arrangement they'd normally keep with us.\n\nDeal with the biggest of them.\n\nAnd before you suggest it, yes, I could send word to the Warchief and ask for the quota to be adjusted on account of bears. I'm told he's very reasonable about this sort of thing.\n\nThat's precisely why I'm asking you instead.",
        'The biggest bears, near the felling line.',
    ),
    'splintertree_blood_orders': (
        "Cultists carry orders. All of them do, and the Burning Blade are no exception, because somebody has to tell them where to stand and when.\n\nAnd a cultist never carries anything he can't hide behind a knife, which means the orders are on them, and they're small, and they'll be destroyed the moment one of them decides a fight is going badly.\n\nSo be quick about it. That's the whole of my tactical advice. Be quick, and go through them before the ink has a chance to meet any blood.\n\nBring me the scraps. I don't care what the orders say, because orders are always the same four sentences. I care about the hand. Somebody is writing these, and a hand I can match is a person I can find.",
        'Get the scraps before the ink meets any blood.',
    ),
    'splintertree_wraiths': (
        "I've buried four recruits this week, four of them, and they were four I trained myself, which I mention not for sympathy but so you understand I'm not reading this off a casualty list.\n\nThere are wraiths in the timber east of the post. They come at the woodcutting details, and the details are exactly the wrong people to meet them: axes, no shields, and half of them are nineteen.\n\nI want them back in the dirt.\n\nAnd don't say it. I've had three separate people explain to me this week that they're already dead, as though that were a clever observation that hadn't occurred to me, and the next one to say it digs the graves.",
        "Put them back in the dirt, and don't tell me they're already dead.",
    ),
    'splintertree_demon_wastes_watch': (
        "From this wall, I see smoke, every night, from somewhere out in the Wastes, and I've logged the position of it the best I can from up here.\n\nFrom the ground, you see who lit it.\n\nThat's the whole difference between my job and the job I'm asking you to do, and I'd swap if I could. But if I come off this wall there's nobody on it, and an empty wall is just a fence.\n\nWalk the edge of the Wastes, and come back and tell me what's burning and who's standing round it.\n\nI'll keep the wall warm. That's a joke. It's the only one I've got, and I've been working on it for two weeks.",
        "Tell me what's burning out there, and who's standing round it.",
    ),
    'splintertree_voidwalkers': (
        "The remnants drift through the Demon Wastes, east of this wall, after dark.\n\nThey aren't alive. They aren't dead either, and I've had that argument with a priest and won it. They weren't made for the world. That's the nearest I can get. Something summoned them here and then stopped paying attention, and they've been bumping along the inside of a place that doesn't want them ever since.\n\nThey're drifting closer to the wall every night. I've marked it.\n\nDisperse them out there, before they get close enough to find out how thin this wall actually is. I know how thin it is. I'd prefer to keep that between us.",
        'Disperse them before they get close enough to test the wall.',
    ),
    'lumber_felling_season': (
        "A good tree dies in four swings. I've timed it, I've trained it, and I've built a camp's whole work rate around it.\n\nThese walkers take it back with a root in the ear.\n\nThe timberlings stand with the stand east of camp, and they don't let a crew near it. They're patient about it. They don't charge. They just put themselves between my boys and the trunk until somebody loses their temper, and then it's a funeral.\n\nClear them off so my crews can work. And don't leave any standing. I've tried leaving a few, and they fetch more.",
        "Don't leave any standing. They fetch more if you do.",
    ),
    'lumber_elder_stump': (
        "There's a stump out there that walks.\n\nI've cut forty years of trees. I've taken things down that were old when my grandfather's clan came over the sea, and I've never minded it. A tree's a tree and a camp's a camp, and I sleep like a stone.\n\nThat one's the oldest thing in this forest, and it guards the last good stand, and I've never in my life wanted something gone so badly.\n\nI'm not going to dress that up as duty. It's the quota, and it's also that it stood and looked at me across the cut for an hour last week and I haven't slept right since.\n\nFell it for me. The camp will drink to it, and I'll be buying. The crews call it the Elder Timberling.",
        'Fell it for me, and the drinks are on me.',
    ),
    'lumber_silk_slings': (
        "Rope breaks, and that isn't a complaint, it's an engineering fact, and every log-sling in this camp is rope, and every one of them will eventually part under a full oak.\n\nSilk doesn't break.\n\nThe forest spiders out here are bigger than they've any business being, and so is their silk. The strands come off them thick as a bootlace, and you can't cut one with a belt knife. I've tried, in front of witnesses.\n\nBring me bundles of it, enough to weave slings with.\n\nI'll lift a whole oak in one piece, and I'll do it in front of the Overseer, who's been telling me for two years that it can't be done with the crew I've got.",
        "Enough for slings. I'll lift a whole oak in front of the Overseer.",
    ),
    'raynewood_cats': (
        "I don't generally welcome strangers to this retreat. I certainly don't generally welcome the Horde, and I'd rather say so to your face than have you find it out in my manner.\n\nThe nightsabers don't distinguish.\n\nThey've stopped respecting the quiet here. Raynewood has kept a boundary for a very long time without a fence. The cats knew where it was and kept to the other side of it, and now they come through it at dusk, and they come as far as the door.\n\nDrive them off.\n\nWhen it's done the retreat can go back to being quiet, and you and I can go back to our respective positions on each other, which I think we'd both prefer.",
        "Drive them back past the boundary, and we'll call it even.",
    ),
    'raynewood_dryads': (
        "The dryads here have gone to sleep. That by itself would be no cause for alarm. They do sleep, deeply, for longer than you'd credit.\n\nWhat wakes in them isn't them.\n\nI've watched it happen twice now. The sleep goes on too long, and then there's movement, and what gets up moves wrong from the first step. There's nothing of the dryad left in it by the time it's standing.\n\nPut them to rest rather than leave them to that. I'd do it myself, and I want that said, but my hands aren't what they used to be, and this isn't work to do badly.\n\nPlease be quick. And not while they're still sleeping, if you can help it. Let them be asleep.",
        'Let them stay asleep, if you can help it.',
    ),
}
